00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.640 align:start position:0%
 
so we recently talked about Trump  Secretary of Education Betsy DeVos  moving to not reimburse certain  defrauded students students who have  been defrauded by the education industry  or world and that's sort of the topic of  today's classic interview we're going  back to October of 2014 when I spoke to  mark Mason who is an expert in corporate  influence over education and we also  talked about standardized testing which  I know is important to others in the  audience as well so let's get to my  interview with mark from 2014 think  about it in the context of what we're  seeing the Trump administration and  Betsy DeVos push now in terms of  education policy I'm joined today by  mark Mason who is a professor of  african-american studies in history at  Fordham University also co-founder of  the badass Teachers Association the  largest education activist group in the  nation the topic today is corporate  influence on education and professor  nation you know I think if you were to  say to many people what is the effect of  corporations on education they might  come up with something like oh is it the  kind of corporatization of college or is  it Doritos and Coca Cola in vending  machines in schools but we're really  talking about something far more  sinister aren't we  absolutely the corporate influence is  shaping what every child will learn from  pre-k through through college and it's  being done largely through influencing  government bodies to impose curricula  that teachers think are burdensome and  inappropriate and sometimes abusive and  the best example of this is the Common  Core Standards which are now being  presented as if they were a bottom-up  initiative by teachers educated and  elected officials organized through the  states when in fact 200mm  million dollars of Bill Gates money was  given to governor's bodies to PTAs to  teachers unions and foundations to try  to push this initiative and when we in  New York State experienced for the first  time test the line to Common Core  teachers parents were and and educators  were just shocked that this had been  imposed on them without any discussion  and also without any ability to modify  them taught a little bit if you can  about the impetus for in other words why  do big corporations want to get involved  in education what is that what is the  ultimate goal well one of them is this  is a huge market it's you know 788  billion dollar market of government  expenditure and education that test  companies technology and software  companies all see as a tremendous market  if you can standardize teaching enough C  you can develop nationwide products for  the market so test companies stand to  make huge profits if there is a national  curriculum that is uniform across the  country so that's one motive is and then  people who are trying to develop a  procedure to have the entire process  digitized hat stand to make a lot of  money from the software but there's  another motivation and that is that a  lot of businesses feel their labor force  is not being properly trained by the  public schools and want to make sure  that there's kind of a uniform level of  of training so that if somebody  graduates from high school you know they  can be employees who can can plug right  into their their companies do you see a  connection or do you see as two  unrelated issues  what you and I have just been talking  about for the last few minutes and the  issue of both the rewriting of textbooks  with a kind of religious impetus or  through religious lens that we're seeing  catalyzed quite a bit in Texas as well  as the fight to teach so-called  controversies around for example  evolution for okay do you see those are  these completely unrelated issues are  completely different initiatives what's  very interesting is that the people  behind Common Core are very much opposed  to those grassroots conservative  initiatives so in if and and if that's  what makes this so complicated initially  the opposition to the sort of top-down  curricular modification was coming a lot  from conservatives and it took a while  for liberals and progressives to realize  wait a minute when big money shapes  education policy that's a subversion of  democracy so there were many issues in  education and one of them is the attempt  to try to undermine you know teaching  that is going on in car in in science  and history to present the more  religious perspective but then there is  an attempt to reshape education policy  from the top-down in a way that really  excludes teachers and parents so we have  a lot on our plate and talk to us a  little bit about once we see or what do  we know I guess is the best way to ask  about what the effects are on students  who go through the education systems  that are so influenced by corporation  what then is the effect when they later  in life enter the workforce for it well  you know this is a very interesting  question I'm a labor historian and I you  know I've done a lot of research in the  1930s when the largest corporation in  the country was General Motors now the  largest corporation in the country is  Walmart which starts off its associates  at 750 an hour even though its CEO  make $16,000 an hour if you are looking  at the kind of jobs we're creating  we're seven out of ten jobs are going to  be entry-level you know moderate to low  wage jobs it seems to me that what we're  doing with a lot of these corporate  policies we're not encouraging critical  thinking we're encouraging rote learning  we're encouraging you know obedience  where in car you know you're not that  you're taking out literature for example  and print putting in sort of you know  very narrow textual analysis you're  removing a lot of studies of history  you're removing hands-on science that is  experimental for things which can be  done on a computer you know it's amazing  as you're saying this and I don't know  if this is my kind of cynical mind going  to a dark place what you're saying in a  way and connecting it to to Walmart as  the biggest employer is reminding me of  when I read the great book brave new  world by Aldous Huxley where there was a  deliberate effort based on the knowledge  that if everybody is very intellectually  curious and fully equipped with critical  thinking and other resources there are  many jobs that they simply would not do  they would not tolerate thus there were  people that were educated or raised in a  particular way to tolerate those jobs  David you are right on target that we I  actually have a former student who is  now a professor at LSU our next book is  going to be on the Walmart ization of  Education what we have is an enforced  standardization from the top down to  reduce education to the lowest common  denominator and take the creativity out  of the classroom and with starting kids  from pre-k we're removing play we're  removing recess all kids do is study for  tests and the tests are expensive so  what gets pushed out when budgets are  strapped or it's music  in school trips you know you're it's  almost like you're making kids adapt to  a brave new world of low paid work where  they have little autonomy and authority  in the last bit of time we have here can  you give our audience some ideas as to  how they can work to counteract lowers  okay the best thing you can do is if you  think testing in your schools are  excessive have your kids refuse to take  the tests there is a growing movement of  test refusal or opting out around the  country and you're talking about the  standardized tests right now not just  tests with a class yes okay I'm also  saying if your kids have recess cancel  to study for tests tell the school  that's unacceptable  tell the schools that there are too many  tests for example in New York State or  third graders take six days of tests 90  minutes a day they take more tests than  you take to get into law school and  medical school I think I go back to you  know the 60s and to me the most horrible  bipartisan initiative in my lifetime  before this was the Vietnam War and  though part of the way we stopped it was  to resist what we thought was an unjust  war now the most destructive bipartisan  initiative is tests based education  policy and I think we have to resist the  tests teachers parents students give you  a number before No Child Left Behind the  average public school gave six tests a  year now they give 17 this is way out of  hand  and the only people can stop it are  parents teachers and older students by  basically saying we won't take the test  unless you cut back and if you're going  to spend money in their schools spend  money and things that make kids love  school which I'm sorry  and also stop scripting teachers and  constantly judging  the basis of test scores and coming in  and observing them on rubrics where you  checkoff little things trust you  teachers to teach all right we've been  speaking with professor mark Mason  professor of african-american studies in  history at Fordham University really a  pleasure to have you on thank you so  much so much I really appreciate the  opportunity to talk about this it's a  very important subject and I think  people don't realize what's going on  until they talk to parents about what's  going on with their kids in school

